            <ArticleTitle>Is personality involved in the expression of dysmenorrhea in patients with endometriosis?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>723-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate whether a relationship exists between personality and expression of pain in women with endometriosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">With use of the Rosenzweig picture frustration study, the personality of women with endometriosis was assessed. These results were then correlated with the manifestation of pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Women without dysmenorrhea tended to be less assertive compared with women who complained of dysmenorrhea and women without endometriosis. Those women without dysmenorrhea showed a destructive attitude in face of a problem.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Personality affected the expression of pain. Pain caused by endometriosis was influenced by each personality type.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
